          "text": "Here is your learning plan:\n\n---\n\n## Learning Plan: ft-polaris Catalog (Commit Critical Path + Fivetran Customizations)\n\nThink of this as five sessions, each building on the last. Since you already own the DLW/Donkey side, you know what the catalog *receives* — this plan focuses on what the catalog *does with it*.\n\n---\n\n### Session 1 — Orient: Module Map & Where DLW Lands\n\n**Goal:** Know which module does what and where your DLW `commitTransaction` call arrives.\n\n**Read:**\n1. `settings.gradle` — see all modules at a glance\n2. `polaris-service/src/main/java/org/apache/polaris/service/catalog/IcebergCatalogAdapter.java` — this is the REST entrypoint. Find `updateTable` and `commitTransaction`. These are the two methods DLW hits.\n3. The REST API spec: `polaris-service/src/main/resources/polaris-management-service.yml` (or the OpenAPI spec files) to see the URL paths.\n\n**Key insight:** The stack is `REST adapter → handler wrapper → base catalog → meta store manager → JPA persistence`. Each layer has a single clear responsibility.\n\n---\n\n### Session 2 — The Commit Critical Path (Deep Dive)\n\n**Goal:** Trace a `POST /v1/{catalog}/transactions/commit` end-to-end.\n\n**Read in order:**\n1. `polaris-service/src/main/java/org/apache/polaris/service/catalog/IcebergCatalogAdapter.java` — `commitTransaction()` method. Note how it calls `newHandlerWrapper()` and then delegates.\n2. `polaris-service/src/main/java/org/apache/polaris/service/catalog/PolarisCatalogHandlerWrapper.java` — `commitTransaction()` around line 986. This is the richest layer. Key things happening here:\n   - **Authorization check** (`authorizeCollectionOfTableLikeOperationOrThrow`) — resolves all tables in the transaction and checks RBAC before touching anything\n   - **TransactionWorkspaceMetaStoreManager** — a in-memory overlay that buffers all changes, allowing multi-table atomicity\n   - For each table update: loads current metadata → validates requirements → applies updates → stages into workspace\n   - Final call to `metaStoreManager.updateEntitiesPropertiesIfNotChanged()` — this is the actual atomic commit (CAS-based)\n3. `polaris-core/src/main/java/org/apache/polaris/core/catalog/BasePolarisCatalog.java` — `BasePolarisTableOperations.doCommit()`. This is where metadata is written to object storage and the entity record is updated.\n4. `extension/persistence/eclipselink/` — the JPA implementation of `PolarisMetaStoreManager`. This is where SQL hits the database.\n\n**The atomic commit contract:** The CAS (`updateEntitiesPropertiesIfNotChanged`) means: \"update only if the entity version hasn't changed since I read it.\" If two writers race, one wins and the other gets a `CommitFailedException` — same semantics as Iceberg's optimistic concurrency.\n\n---\n\n### Session 3 — Storage Credential Vending\n\n**Goal:** Understand how the catalog mints short-lived credentials so DLW (or Spark) can read/write actual Parquet files.\n\n**Read in order:**\n1. `polaris-core/src/main/java/org/apache/polaris/core/storage/cache/StorageCredentialCache.java` — the in-memory cache (recently increased from 10K to 30K entries, see commit `c612bd11`).\n2. `polaris-core/src/main/java/org/apache/polaris/core/storage/azure/AzureCredentialsStorageIntegration.java` — the most Fivetran-customized integration. Look for `fetchSecretWithRetry()` and the SECRED imports.\n3. `polaris-core/src/main/java/org/apache/polaris/core/storage/aws/` — STS assume-role for S3.\n4. `polaris-core/src/main/java/org/apache/polaris/core/storage/gcp/` — service account impersonation for GCS. Note the recent fix for `ImpersonatedCredentials` 503 retry (commit `9ca0bfad`).\n5. `BasePolarisCatalog.getCredentialConfig()` and `refreshCredentials()` — how the catalog assembles credentials before returning a `LoadTableResponse`.\n\n**The flow from DLW's perspective:** DLW calls `loadTable` → catalog returns table metadata + scoped credentials → DLW uses credentials directly against object storage → DLW stages new data files → DLW calls `commitTransaction` with file additions → catalog updates metadata only (no data movement).\n\n---\n\n### Session 4 — Fivetran Customizations vs Upstream Polaris\n\n**Goal:** Know exactly what Fivetran added or changed so you can reason about upgrade compatibility.\n\n**Fivetran-specific pieces:**\n\n| What | File(s) | Why it exists |\n|---|---|---|\n| **SECRED integration** | `PolarisStorageIntegrationProviderImpl.java`, `AzureCredentialsStorageIntegration.java` | Azure token vending via Fivetran's internal secrets service instead of ambient identity |\n| **`StorageUtil.isFivetranEnvSet()`** | `polaris-core/.../StorageUtil.java` | Feature flag to activate Fivetran-specific code paths |\n| **`PolarisApplication` SECRED retry setup** | `PolarisApplication.java` lines 136-139 | Resilient startup when SECRED is briefly unavailable |\n| **`StorageCredentialCache` 30K limit** | `StorageCredentialCache.java` | Scaled up for Fivetran's multi-tenant load (commit `c612bd11`) |\n| **`PolarisS3FileIOClientFactory`** | `polaris-core/.../aws/PolarisS3FileIOClientFactory.java` | Custom S3 client factory wired into `BasePolarisCatalog.loadFileIO()` |\n| **`VersionBlockingFilter`** | `polaris-service/.../filter/VersionBlockingFilter.java` | Blocks legacy `polaris-09` protocol versions |\n| **GCP 503 retry for `ImpersonatedCredentials`** | `GcpCredentialsStorageIntegration.java` | Upstream didn't handle wrapped exceptions; Fivetran fixed it (commit `9ca0bfad`) |\n| **GAR token auto-fetch** | `build-logic/` | Gradle plugin to auto-fetch Google Artifact Registry token (commit `fd228054`) |\n\n**What's upstream Polaris:** The RBAC model, `BasePolarisCatalog`, `PolarisCatalogHandlerWrapper`, all persistence abstractions, the Iceberg REST spec compliance, JPA/EclipseLink persistence backend.\n\n---\n\n### Session 5 — Authorization & Multi-Tenancy\n\n**Goal:** Understand how Polaris isolates tenants and enforces permissions — relevant when DLW commits on behalf of a customer.\n\n**Read:**\n1. `polaris-core/src/main/java/org/apache/polaris/core/auth/PolarisAuthorizerImpl.java` — RBAC evaluation. The key model: principals have principal-roles, catalog-roles have grants on catalog objects, the authorizer checks if the principal's active roles transitively hold the required grant.\n2. `PolarisCatalogHandlerWrapper` — `authorizeCollectionOfTableLikeOperationOrThrow()`. Note that for `commitTransaction`, it pre-resolves *all* tables in the transaction before doing any work — the whole transaction is authorized or rejected atomically.\n3. `polaris-service/src/main/java/org/apache/polaris/service/auth/` — how OAuth2 bearer tokens are validated and mapped to Polaris principals.\n\n---\n\n### Recommended Reading Order (Condensed)\n\nIf you only have time for one pass, read these five files in sequence — they cover the entire critical path:\n\n1. `IcebergCatalogAdapter.java` — REST entry\n2. `PolarisCatalogHandlerWrapper.java` — auth + transaction orchestration  \n3. `BasePolarisCatalog.java` — catalog semantics + credential vending\n4. `AzureCredentialsStorageIntegration.java` — deepest Fivetran customization\n5. EclipseLink `PolarisMetaStoreManagerImpl.java` (in `extension/persistence/eclipselink/`) — persistence\n\n---\n\n### Open Questions to Investigate As You Go\n\n- How does Polaris know which storage config (S3/GCS/Azure) applies to a given table location? (Look at `PolarisStorageConfigInfo` and how it's stored as entity properties.)\n- What happens when SECRED is down? Is there a fallback or does `commitTransaction` fail hard for Azure tables?\n- How does the `TransactionWorkspaceMetaStoreManager` guarantee that its in-memory workspace is cleaned up on failure/success?",
