          "type": "assistant",
          "text": "Here are the answers to all four questions:\n\n---\n\n## What is a \"realm\"?\n\nA realm is the top-level **tenant isolation boundary** in Polaris. Think of it as \"which customer's universe does this request belong to?\" Each realm has its own completely separate persistence layer — its own database schema or store — with its own principals, catalogs, namespaces, and tables. Two realms share no data and cannot see each other.\n\nIn practice, `DefaultContextResolver` extracts the realm identifier from the incoming request's `Authorization: Bearer` token (or a header fallback). That identifier is then used to look up or create the right `PolarisMetaStoreSession` for that tenant (`metaStoreManagerFactory.getOrCreateSessionSupplier(realmContext).get()`). So the realm is how a single Polaris deployment serves many independent customers.\n\n---\n\n## What is a \"call\"? What is `CallContext`'s lifecycle?\n\nYes — a \"call\" is exactly one HTTP request. `CallContext` is born and dies inside the servlet filter in `PolarisApplication.doFilter()`:\n\n```\n→ request arrives\n  realmContextResolver.resolveRealmContext(...)   // parse realm from token/headers\n  callContextResolver.resolveCallContext(...)     // open a DB session for that realm\n  CallContext.setCurrentContext(currentCallContext)  // install on thread\n  chain.doFilter(request, response)              // entire request processing happens here\n  // finally:\n  currentCallContext.close()                     // unset thread-local, close DB session + FileIO\n```\n\nThe `close()` method does two things: it removes the context from the thread-local, and it closes the `CloseableGroup` — which holds things like the `FileIO` instances and the database session that were opened during the request. The request handler never manages these resources directly; it just registers them into `callContext.closeables()` and the filter cleans up.\n\n---\n\n## Why a thread-local? Does the context change during a request?\n\nThe context does **not** change mid-request on the same thread. The thread-local is set once at filter entry and cleared at filter exit.\n\nThe thread-local exists because deep layers like `BasePolarisCatalog` need the context without it being threaded through every method parameter. This is a standard Java web server pattern — Spring uses `RequestContextHolder` the same way, Hibernate uses it for sessions. It's ambient state scoped to the request thread.\n\nThe reason it's `InheritableThreadLocal` (not plain `ThreadLocal`) is the background task case in `TaskExecutorImpl`:\n\n```java\n// TaskExecutorImpl.java:83\ntry (CallContext ctx = CallContext.setCurrentContext(CallContext.copyOf(clone))) {\n```\n\nWhen a request spawns an async background task on a different thread, that task needs its own context (same realm, same configuration, but its own DB session and its own `closeables` lifecycle). `copyOf` creates a fresh context that shares the realm/principal info but excludes the parent's `CLOSEABLES` — so the task manages its own resource cleanup independently. `InheritableThreadLocal` means that if a child thread is created without an explicit `setCurrentContext`, it at least inherits a copy of the parent's value rather than getting null, which is safer.\n\nSo the design is: one context per execution context (thread), not one context per request across all threads. Each thread that does work sets its own context and is responsible for cleaning it up.",
