          "text": "An entity is the **universal unit of catalog metadata** in Polaris — every named, persistent thing that Polaris tracks is an entity. Looking at `PolarisEntityType`, the complete list is:\n\n```\nROOT (virtual, never stored — the conceptual root of the tree)\n├── PRINCIPAL          (a service account or user)\n├── PRINCIPAL_ROLE     (a role assignable to principals)\n├── CATALOG            (an Iceberg catalog)\n│   ├── CATALOG_ROLE   (a role granting access within a catalog)\n│   └── NAMESPACE      (can be nested — parentSelfReference = true)\n│       └── TABLE_LIKE (subtype TABLE or subtype VIEW)\n│           └── FILE\n└── TASK               (background async work)\n```\n\n---\n\n## What an entity record actually holds\n\nEvery entity shares these fields from `PolarisEntityCore` + `PolarisBaseEntity`:\n\n| Field | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `catalogId` + `id` | Unique identity within the realm. Top-level entities (CATALOG, PRINCIPAL) use `catalogId = 0`. |\n| `parentId` | Points to the parent entity's `id`, forming the tree. A table's parent is a namespace; a namespace's parent is another namespace or the catalog. |\n| `typeCode` / `subTypeCode` | What kind of entity. `TABLE_LIKE` uses subtype to distinguish `TABLE` vs `VIEW`. |\n| `name` | Human-readable name, unique within the parent. |\n| `entityVersion` | Monotonically incrementing integer. Bumped on every data change. Used for CAS — the commit \"wins\" only if the version matches what you read. |\n| `grantRecordsVersion` | A separate version counter, bumped whenever a grant is added or removed on this entity. Tracked separately because permission changes are frequent and independent from data changes. |\n| `properties` | JSON blob of user-visible key/value properties (what DLW or Spark sees, like `write.format.default`). |\n| `internalProperties` | JSON blob of internal Polaris-only metadata, not exposed to clients (storage config type, base location, and — most importantly for tables — the **Iceberg metadata file location**). |\n| `dropTimestamp` / `purgeTimestamp` | Soft-delete lifecycle: drop marks the entity, purge eventually removes it and its backing data. |\n\n---\n\n## The critical distinction: entity ≠ Iceberg table metadata\n\nThis trips up most people coming from the DLW side. An entity record in Polaris is **not** the Iceberg table metadata (the `metadata.json` with schemas, partition specs, snapshots, manifest lists, data files). That lives in object storage and can be megabytes large.\n\nThe entity record is just the **registry pointer** — essentially:\n\n```\n\"Table 'orders' in catalog 'prod', namespace 'sales', lives at\n s3://bucket/warehouse/sales/orders/metadata/00042-abc.metadata.json\"\n```\n\nThat metadata location is stored in `internalProperties`. When `BasePolarisTableOperations.doRefresh()` runs, it reads the entity to get the metadata location, then reads the actual Iceberg JSON from object storage. When `doCommit()` runs, it writes new Iceberg metadata JSON to storage, then updates the entity's `internalProperties` with the new path — that's the atomic step protected by `entityVersion` CAS.\n\nSo from DLW's perspective: DLW writes Parquet data files and a new Iceberg `metadata.json` to object storage, then calls `commitTransaction` — and what Polaris actually commits to its database is just an entity update containing the new metadata file path and the bumped `entityVersion`.",
