          "text": "Good question. Let me build up from first principles.\n\n---\n\n## Cloud IAM in one paragraph\n\nEvery cloud vendor (AWS, GCP, Azure) has an Identity and Access Management (IAM) system. In IAM, a **role** is a named set of permissions (e.g. \"can read from S3 bucket X\", \"can write to GCS bucket Y\"). A **service account** or **IAM role** is assigned to a process or application. When that process wants to access a cloud resource, it presents a **credential** (a token, a key, a signed certificate) that proves \"I am this identity.\" The cloud vendor checks: does this identity's role permit this operation on this resource? If yes, allow. If no, deny.\n\n---\n\n## What is a \"scoped credential\"?\n\nA scoped credential is a **short-lived token minted from a broader credential, but with its permissions intentionally narrowed down**.\n\nThink of it like a hotel key card. The hotel's master key opens every door. But when you check in, they give you a key card that only opens room 412 and the gym — same hotel, much narrower access. If you lose that key card, the damage is limited.\n\nIn cloud terms: Polaris itself runs with a powerful IAM role (the \"master key\") that can access many customers' storage locations. When DLW needs to read or write files for a specific table, Polaris mints a **scoped credential** — a short-lived token that can *only* access that table's specific storage paths, for a limited time (typically 15 minutes to 1 hour). DLW uses that token directly against object storage, not via Polaris.\n\n---\n\n## Why `allowListOperation`, `allowedReadLocations`, `allowedWriteLocations`?\n\nHere's the key insight: **the credential vendor (Polaris) doesn't decide what the caller is trying to do — it decides what the minted token is *capable* of doing.** The caller tells Polaris \"I need to read from these paths and write to these paths\", and Polaris encodes exactly those permissions into the token it mints.\n\nEach parameter narrows the token:\n\n- **`allowedReadLocations`** — \"mint a token that can read from these specific paths\" (e.g. `s3://bucket/warehouse/sales/orders/data/`)\n- **`allowedWriteLocations`** — \"mint a token that can write to these paths\"\n- **`allowListOperation`** — \"also allow listing directory contents\" (needed to discover existing data files; often kept separate because list access is broader than read access)\n\nThe reason the *caller* provides these is that Polaris doesn't know what the caller is about to do. DLW knows it needs to write new Parquet files to a specific partition path and read existing ones — so it tells Polaris exactly that. Polaris's job is to:\n1. Verify that the authenticated caller is *authorized* to access those locations at all (RBAC check against the catalog's storage config)\n2. Mint a cloud token scoped to exactly those paths and operations\n\nThis principle is called **least-privilege**: the token carries the minimum permissions needed for the task. If the token is stolen or leaked, the attacker can only access those specific paths during that short window — not the entire storage bucket or every customer's data.\n\n---\n\n## How it works per cloud\n\n**AWS (STS AssumeRole):** Polaris calls AWS STS (Security Token Service) with an IAM policy document that explicitly lists allowed S3 paths and actions (`s3:GetObject`, `s3:PutObject`, `s3:ListBucket`). STS returns a temporary `(access_key_id, secret_access_key, session_token)` triple that is only valid for the specified paths and a short duration.\n\n**GCP (Service Account impersonation):** Polaris impersonates a service account and generates a short-lived OAuth token scoped to specific GCS bucket paths.\n\n**Azure (SAS token):** Polaris generates a Shared Access Signature token (the SECRED integration you saw earlier) that encodes permitted operations and paths directly into the signed URL/token string.\n\nIn all three cases, the cloud vendor enforces the scope — DLW cannot use the token to access a