          "text": "The idiomatic pattern in this codebase is **`PolarisConfigurationStore`** — a dynamic key/value store read at request time. Here's how it's used.\n\n## How existing code does it\n\nExample from `InMemoryStorageIntegration.java:84-85`:\n\n```java\npc.getConfigurationStore().getConfiguration(pc, \"ALLOW_WILDCARD_LOCATION\", false)\n```\n\nYou pass the `PolarisCallContext`, the key name, and a default. The store returns the configured value if present, else the default.\n\nValues are supplied via the Dropwizard YAML config file (parsed in `PolarisApplicationConfig.getConfigurationStore()` into `DefaultConfigurationStore`). The store supports **per-realm overrides** too — same mechanism used for tenant-level feature gating.\n\n## Honest caveat on \"online\"\n\nThe YAML is loaded **once at startup**. Changing a flag requires editing the config and restarting the Polaris process. There's no hot-reload mechanism in `DefaultConfigurationStore`. So \"turn off online\" really means \"redeploy with the flag flipped\" — which in Fivetran's deployment model is fast (Kubernetes rollout), but still not instant.\n\nIf you need a true zero-restart kill switch (seconds, not minutes), you'd need to integrate a remote flag service. That's out of scope for this PR — the YAML flag is the right default.\n\n## Proposed integration for our change\n\nPick a key name — something like `ENABLE_AZURE_SECRED_CACHE`. Default to `true`.\n\nWrap the two cache call sites in `AzureCredentialsStorageIntegration`:\n\n```java\nprivate boolean isCacheEnabled() {\n  PolarisCallContext pc = CallContext.getCurrentContext().getPolarisCallContext();\n  return pc.getConfigurationStore().getConfiguration(pc, \"ENABLE_AZURE_SECRED_CACHE\", true);\n}\n```\n\n**Call site 1 — `getOrFetchSecret()`:**\n\n```java\nUnifiedDataLakeCredentials credential =\n    isCacheEnabled()\n        ? secredCredCache.getOrFetch(catalogName,\n              key -> fetchSecretWithRetry(key, credentialServiceRef, credentialServiceFactory))\n        : fetchSecretWithRetry(catalogName, credentialServiceRef, credentialServiceFactory);\n```\n\n**Call site 2 — reactive-invalidation path in `getSubscopedCreds()`:**\n\n```java\nif (secredCredCache != null\n    && catalogName != null\n    && isCacheEnabled()              // NEW\n    && isInvalidClientSecretError(ex)\n    && !secredRefreshAttempted) {\n  // ...invalidate + refresh\n}\n```\n\nWhen the flag is off: every request goes directly to SECRED (original pre-change behavior), and the reactive-refresh path becomes a no-op.\n\n## To turn it off\n\nAdd to the Polaris YAML config (ask someone who operates the service for the exact file path — check `k8s/` or deployment configs):\n\n```yaml\nfeatureConfiguration:\n  ENABLE_AZURE_SECRED_CACHE: false\n```\n\nOr for per-realm rollout:\n\n```yaml\nrealmConfiguration:\n  realm-having-issues:\n    ENABLE_AZURE_SECRED_CACHE: false\n```\n\nRedeploy. The flag is read per request, so the next request picks up the new value.\n\n## Two points worth deciding with the team\n\n1. **Default value.** `true` ships with the feature on and requires an incident to toggle off. `false` ships off and requires a follow-up deploy to enable. For a cache that doesn't affect correctness (only latency + SECRED load), `true` is the usual choice.\n2. **Should the flag be realm-scoped or global?** Realm-scoped is cheap to add and gives you canary capability. I'd include realm-scoping from the start since `DefaultConfigurationStore` supports it for free.\n\nWant me to wire the flag through the two call sites?",
