          "text": "Yes, fully feasible, and Polaris already does exactly this pattern for another feature.\n\n## Existing precedent in Polaris\n\n**`VersionBlockingFilter.java`** is the closest analogue — a `ContainerRequestFilter` that reads a custom header (`x-version`) and switches behavior based on its value. It rejects rather than branches, but the shape is identical: per-request, header-driven, no restart required to change.\n\nOther places where headers already drive behavior:\n- `DefaultContextResolver.resolveRealmContext(...)` / `resolveCallContext(...)` — receives `Map<String, String> headers` and uses them to select the realm identity.\n- `TracingFilter.java:71` — reads `Polaris-Client-ID` header for trace correlation.\n- `PolarisApplication.doFilter` — reads `x-request-id` for logging correlation.\n\nSo headers → per-request behavior is a first-class pattern in this codebase. Your colleagues' approach is aligned with what's already there.\n\n## Why this works for \"turn off online\"\n\nThe flag lives in the upstream service (DLW or similar) that already has a real online flag system (LaunchDarkly or equivalent). That service reads the flag for each outgoing request and forwards the value in a header. Polaris doesn't need any local flag store — it just trusts and reads the header. Flipping the flag in the upstream system propagates to Polaris on the next request. No Polaris restart needed.\n\n## Implementation shape\n\nThe cleanest fit, mirroring `VersionBlockingFilter`:\n\n**Step 1 — filter reads the header once per request and stashes it:**\n\n```java\n@Provider\npublic class FeatureFlagsFilter implements ContainerRequestFilter {\n  static final String CACHE_FLAG_HEADER = \"x-polaris-azure-secred-cache-enabled\";\n\n  @Override\n  public void filter(ContainerRequestContext ctx) {\n    String v = ctx.getHeaderString(CACHE_FLAG_HEADER);\n    FeatureFlags.setAzureSecredCacheEnabled(\n        v == null ? true : Boolean.parseBoolean(v));   // default on\n  }