            "new_string": "## Configuration\n\nGate the new behavior behind a per-request feature flag so it can be toggled online — without a Polaris restart — if a regression surfaces in production.\n\n### Mechanism: HTTP-header-driven per-request flag\n\nThe flag lives in an upstream service (e.g., DLW) that already has an online flag system (LaunchDarkly or equivalent). That service reads the flag for each outgoing request to Polaris and forwards the current value in a custom HTTP header. Polaris reads the header per request to decide which code path to take. Flipping the flag in the upstream system propagates to Polaris on the next request with no deploy or restart required.\n\nThis mirrors the existing Polaris pattern used by `VersionBlockingFilter` (reads `x-version` header to reject specific client versions), `TracingFilter` (reads `Polaris-Client-ID`), and `DefaultContextResolver` (reads headers to resolve realm). Header → per-request behavior is a first-class pattern in this codebase.\n\n### Header\n\n- Header name: `x-polaris-azure-secred-cache-enabled`\n- Header values: `true` or `false`\n- Default when header is absent: `true` (feature enabled). This makes the cache active for any caller that doesn't know about the header yet.\n\n### Components to add\n\n**1. Filter that reads the header per request.** Mirror `polaris-service/src/main/java/org/apache/polaris/service/filter/VersionBlockingFilter.java`.\n\n```java\n// polaris-service/src/main/java/org/apache/polaris/service/filter/FeatureFlagsFilter.java\n@Provider\npublic class FeatureFlagsFilter implements ContainerRequestFilter {\n  static final String CACHE_FLAG_HEADER = \"x-polaris-azure-secred-cache-enabled\";\n\n  @Override\n  public void filter(ContainerRequestContext ctx) {\n    String v = ctx.getHeaderString(CACHE_FLAG_HEADER);\n    FeatureFlags.setAzureSecredCacheEnabled(v == null ? true : Boolean.parseBoolean(v));\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**2. Per-request state holder (thread-local).**\n\n```java\n// polaris-core/src/main/java/org/apache/polaris/core/feature/FeatureFlags.java\npublic final class FeatureFlags {\n  private static final ThreadLocal<Boolean> AZURE_SECRED_CACHE =\n      ThreadLocal.withInitial(() -> true);\n\n  public static boolean isAzureSecredCacheEnabled() { return AZURE_SECRED_CACHE.get(); }\n  static void setAzureSecredCacheEnabled(boolean v) { AZURE_SECRED_CACHE.set(v); }\n  public static void clear() { AZURE_SECRED_CACHE.remove(); }\n}\n```\n\nThe `ThreadLocal.withInitial(() -> true)` gives safe defaults for code paths that run outside a servlet request (bootstrap, background tasks, tests).\n\n**3. Thread-local cleanup at request end.** To prevent leaking a flag value from one request into the next on a reused thread-pool thread, clear in the same servlet-filter `finally` block that already clears `CallContext`. Find the `finally` in `PolarisApplication.doFilter` that calls `CallContext.unsetCurrentContext()` and add `FeatureFlags.clear();` beside it.\n\n### Decision sites in `AzureCredentialsStorageIntegration`\n\n**Site 1 — `getOrFetchSecret()` initial load:**\n\n```java\nUnifiedDataLakeCredentials credential =\n    FeatureFlags.isAzureSecredCacheEnabled()\n        ? secredCredCache.getOrFetch(catalogName,\n              key -> fetchSecretWithRetry(key, credentialServiceRef, credentialServiceFactory))\n        : fetchSecretWithRetry(catalogName, credentialServiceRef, credentialServiceFactory);\n```\n\n**Site 2 — reactive invalidation path in `getSubscopedCreds()`:**\n\n```java\nif (secredCredCache != null\n    && catalogName != null\n    && FeatureFlags.isAzureSecredCacheEnabled()    // NEW\n    && isInvalidClientSecretError(ex)\n    && !secredRefreshAttempted) {\n  // ...invalidate + refresh\n}\n```\n\nWhen the flag is off, every request goes directly to SECRED (original pre-change behavior), and the reactive cache-refresh path becomes a no-op.\n\n### Upstream integration\n\nOut of scope for this Polaris PR, but worth coordinating with the upstream team:\n\n- Upstream service defines a flag in its online flag system (default `true`).\n- Upstream service reads it in the Polaris client layer and adds the header to every outgoing request.\n- To disable online: flip the flag upstream. Polaris picks up the new value on the next request.\n\n### Security / trust model\n\nAny caller that sends this header can toggle the behavior for its own request. Acceptable today because the Polaris → DLW path is internal. If Polaris ever starts serving external traffic, the filter must either:\n\n- Ignore the header unless the request comes from a trusted source (mTLS client cert, internal CIDR, upstream-proxy assertion), or\n- Move the flag mechanism from request-header to something server-side (YAML config via `PolarisConfigurationStore`, or a polling integration with an online flag system inside Polaris itself).\n\n### Testing\n\n- **`FeatureFlagsFilterTest`** — verify the filter correctly maps header presence/absence/value to the thread-local.\n- **`AzureCredentialsStorageIntegrationSpec`** — add cases asserting that when the flag is `false`, `secredCredCache.getOrFetch` is not invoked and `fetchSecretWithRetry` is called directly. Use `FeatureFlags.setAzureSecredCacheEnabled(false)` in the `@BeforeEach` / per-test setup, and `FeatureFlags.clear()` in `@AfterEach` to avoid cross-test pollution.\n\n### Alternative considered: YAML-based `PolarisConfigurationStore`\n\nPolaris has a built-in `PolarisConfigurationStore` pattern (see `ALLOW_WILDCARD_LOCATION` usage in `InMemoryStorageIntegration`) that reads flags from the Dropwizard YAML config. Rejected because the YAML is loaded once at startup — disabling the flag would require a Polaris redeploy, not a true \"online toggle.\" The header-based approach gives real zero-restart toggling by delegating the flag store to an upstream service that already has one.",
