java`, so reuse that same import for consistency within the azure package.\n\n## Where the \"annotate private\" idea might come from\n\nIf your engineering repo actually allows this, one of these is true:\n\n1. **Custom build plugin** — Some orgs have in-house bytecode manipulation (e.g., a Gradle/Maven plugin that flips `private` to package-private for test compilation). Unusual but possible.\n2. **Kotlin, not Java** — Kotlin's `internal` and some testing frameworks (like `@InternalApi` + a test-only classpath) can relax visibility for test modules. If you were writing Kotlin, this would work differently.\n3. **Misremembered** — the methods were actually package-private all along, and only the `@VisibleForTesting` stuck in memory.\n\nPolaris has neither (1) nor (2), so the plain \"drop `private` + `@VisibleForTesting`\" pattern is what you want here.",
