          "text": "Caffeine's docs are confusingly worded on this. The clear answer is:\n\n**You already have eviction enabled by virtue of calling `.maximumSize(...)`.** No additional configuration needed.\n\n## What the doc snippets actually mean\n\n- \"By default cache instances created by Caffeine will not perform any type of eviction\" — true if you create a cache with `Caffeine.newBuilder().build()` and nothing else. Plain `newBuilder()` gives you an unbounded cache that never evicts.\n- \"These features are all optional\" — the eviction features (size-based, weight-based, time-based, reference-based) are all opt-in. You enable each by calling its builder method.\n- \"least-recently-used eviction when a maximum size is exceeded\" — this is the trigger for size-based eviction. It happens automatically once you've called `.maximumSize(N)`.\n\nSo calling `.maximumSize(2048)` is what flipped the switch. You don't need a separate \"enable LRU\" call. Once size > 2048, Caffeine evicts entries until it's back under the limit.\n\n## But it's not actually LRU\n\nThe Caffeine doc says \"least-recently-used eviction\" as a simplification. The real algorithm is **Window TinyLFU**, a frequency-aware policy that:\n\n- Tracks both **recency** (how recently used) and **frequency** (how often used).\n- Uses a tiny LRU \"window\" for new entries plus a frequency-counted main region for established entries.\n- Empirically achieves higher hit rates than pure LRU on most workloads — that's the whole reason Caffeine became popular.\n\nIf you specifically need *pure* LRU semantics (for instance because you're modeling some downstream behavior that depends on it), Caffeine isn't the right tool. For a credential cache, Window TinyLFU is strictly better than LRU and is the default behavior — no configuration needed.\n\n## What this means for your cache\n\nYour setup:\n\n```java\nCaffeine.newBuilder()\n    .maximumSize(2048)                                 // size-based eviction on\n    .recordStats()\n    .expireAfterWrite(1h, TimeUnit.HOURS)              // time-based eviction on\n    .build(...);\n```\n\nYou have **two eviction policies stacked**:\n\n1. **Time-based**: any entry older than 1 hour gets evicted on next access (or via background maintenance).\n2. **Size-based**: if the cache exceeds 2048 entries, Window TinyLFU picks the least-valuable entries to evict.\n\nIn practice, for your workload — a SECRED catalog credential cache where the cardinality is the number of Azure-using catalogs across the deployment (likely hundreds, max thousands) — the size cap is a safety net you'll never hit. Time-based expiry will dominate. The 2048 number is essentially just a memory-bound guarantee in case of some pathological rapid-creation scenario.\n\n## TL;DR\n\n- Yes, `.maximumSize(2048)` already enables eviction.\n- It's Window TinyLFU, not strict LRU. That's a feature, not a bug — better hit rate.\n- You don't need to add anything to make eviction work.\n- For your workload, eviction is mostly hypothetical anyway because the time-based expiry kicks first.",
