he` (a fresh empty one), which is fine for test isolation.\n\n## So your concern: \"the second constructor isn't `@Inject`-annotated, how does it work?\"\n\nIt works because **only one constructor needs the `@Inject` annotation** for CDI to know how to construct the bean. CDI never calls the second constructor directly. The second constructor:\n\n1. Is reachable from production code only via the first constructor's `this(...)` delegation.\n2. Is reachable from tests via direct `new` calls.\n\nCDI doesn't even care that it exists. The annotation just tells CDI \"use this one when you need to make me.\" Other constructors are for whoever wants to call them with the full parameter list.\n\n## Why structure it as two constructors at all?\n\nLook at the two parameter sets:\n\n- 4-arg `@Inject` ctor: `(StorageConfiguration, StsClientProvider, Clock, AzureSecredCredentialCache)` — high-level beans CDI knows how to produce.\n- 4-arg raw ctor: `(StsClientProvider, Optional<AwsCredentialsProvider>, Supplier<GoogleCredentials>, AzureSecredCredentialCache)` — primitive collaborators.\n\nThe first form is convenient for CDI because each parameter is itself a managed bean. The second form is convenient for tests because mocks are easier to construct as `Optional.empty()` and a lambda `() -> GoogleCredentials.create(...)` than to build a full `StorageConfiguration` mock.\n\nThe first constructor essentially does the \"extract the primitives from the high-level beans\" adaptation, then delegates. It's the standard CDI-vs-test escape hatch pattern in Quarkus codebases.\n\n## How would it break?\n\nIf you removed the `@Inject` annotation from the first constructor, CDI wouldn't know which constructor to use and would fail at startup with an \"ambiguous constructor\" or \"no constructor to use\" error. The annotation is what disambiguates, even though both are public.\n\nIf you added `@Inject` to both, CDI would also fail (only one `@Inject` constructor allowed per bean).",
